---
# HOW MANY BYTES A PAGE HANDS THE VISITOR.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FAILURE THIS EXISTS FOR
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# On 2026-09-04 `/uk/london/movies` was taking 7-22 SECONDS to load over a normal connection, and
# every dashboard said the web tier was healthy. Both readings were correct: the server produced
# the response header in ~50 ms and then handed the visitor 1.27 MB of gzipped HTML. Latency
# metrics stop at the response header -- deliberately, because the SSE streams would otherwise put
# a multi-minute tail in the histogram -- so THE ENTIRE COST WAS INVISIBLE. It was found by
# somebody curling the page by hand.
#
# It is not one page and it is not the worst one. Measured the same day, gzipped:
#
#   /us/los-angeles/movies  1.66 MB   70,209 showtimes   <- worst
#   /us/new-york/movies     1.31 MB   50,739 showtimes
#   /uk/london/movies       1.27 MB   28,710 showtimes
#   /us/chicago/movies      1.17 MB   42,780 showtimes
#   /poznan/                0.21 MB    2,224 showtimes   <- typical
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A SIZE RULE AND NOT A LATENCY ONE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# web-latency.rules cannot see this and no tuning of it could. It reads
# `kinowo_web_http_request_duration_seconds`, which by construction measures time-to-header; on
# these exact pages that is ~50 ms. A size histogram is a different question about the same
# request, and it is the one a visitor on a train is actually asking.
#
# THE PAGE HAS NO UPPER BOUND BY CONSTRUCTION. The listing renders the whole city repertoire --
# every film, every cinema, every showtime, each with its booking link -- so weight tracks the
# market. Los Angeles is 33x Poznan's showtime count today, and a new metro is onboarded by adding
# a row to a roster. That is why this alerts on an ABSOLUTE size rather than on growth: there is no
# baseline to grow from, only the size the corpus happens to be.
groups:
  - name: web-page-weight
    rules:
      - alert: WebPageTooHeavy
        # p95 rather than max: one enormous response is a curiosity, but a 95th percentile past the
        # threshold means it is what people are routinely being handed.
        expr: >-
          (
            histogram_quantile(0.95,
              sum by (le, country, route) (
                rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_bucket{
                  route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|/api/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks|sitemap).*"
                }[30m])
              )
            ) > 1048576
          )
          and on (country, route)
          (
            sum by (country, route) (
              rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count[30m])
            ) >= 0.05
          )
        # An hour, because this is a property of the corpus rather than an event. It moves when a
        # city's repertoire moves -- the weekly programme drop, a new market -- and none of those
        # want paging within minutes.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} {{ $labels.route }} is serving {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B pages"
          description: >-
            This route's 95th-percentile response is over a megabyte on the wire, AFTER gzip. The
            server is probably answering fast -- check web-latency.rules, which will read healthy --
            because the cost is in the transfer, not the render. The cause is almost always the
            listing rendering an entire city's repertoire in one document: measure it with
            `curl -H 'Accept-Encoding: gzip' -o /dev/null -w '%{size_download}'` and count
            `data-time="` occurrences for the showtime total. The fix is to bound what is rendered,
            not to compress harder -- gzip is already on, and the repeated markup compresses well
            enough that trimming attributes measured 1.4% while rendering 60 films instead of 784
            measured 80%.
